   165	
   166	**2.1** **Studies on Developers Information Seeking**
   167	
   168	
   169	In every phase of modern software engineering, developers need to
   170	work with unfamiliar code and domains, and how well they learn
   171	such code influences their productivity significantly. Therefore,
   172	researchers have studied to understand how developers learn and
   173	comprehend unfamiliar code and domains [ 30, 38, 51, 62 ], especially
   174	how they search for and acquire information, as developers need a
   175	variety of kinds of knowledge [ 43, 66, 68, 77 ]. Particularly, lots of
   176	research was done on the information seeking strategies of developers, mostly in general software maintenance [ 18, 30, 35 ] or web
   177	search settings [ 7, 59 ]. Researchers have also studied challenges
   178	developers face [ 14, 23, 32, 58, 72, 74 ], including difficulties in effective search-query writing and information foraging, and built
   179	tools to support developers overcome such challenges [ 3, 42, 52 ]. In
   180	this work, we explore a way of supporting developers’ information
   181	needs with generation-based information support using LLMs.
   182	Other efforts were made to understand developers’ information
   183	seeking within software documentation, which is the main source
   184	of information for developers when they learn to use new APIs
   185	or libraries. Researchers cataloged problems developers face when
   186	using documentation [ 9, 60, 61 ] and identified types of knowledge
   187	developers report to be critical [ 46, 60, 61, 70 ], which were taken
   188	into account when we designed GILT for our study.
