   192	
   193	
   194	The potential and applicability of LLM-based AI programming
   195	tools have been actively studied by many researchers. Numerous
   196	empirical studies [ 17, 22, 37, 65 ] evaluated the quality of code or
   197	explanations generated by LLMs, to test the feasibility of applying
   198	LLM into development tools [ 41, 69, 79 ] and to Computer Science
   199	
   200	
   201	
   202	education [ 1, 22, 65 ]. Several studies have also compared LLMgenerated code and explanations with those authored by humans
   203	without LLM assistance [ 22, 37, 56 ], demonstrating that LLMs can
   204	offer reasonably good help for developers or students with caution.
   205	Fewer studies have specifically explored the _usefulness_ of LLMbased programming tools [ 2, 29, 40, 49, 63, 64, 71, 75, 79, 79 ] with
   206	actual users or their usage data, and many of these studies have
   207	focused on code generation tools like CoPilot [ 26 ]. For instance,
   208	Ziegler et al.[ 79 ] analyzed telemetry data and survey responses
   209	to understand developers’ perceived productivity with GitHub
   210	Copilot, revealing that over one-fifth of suggestions were accepted
   211	by actual developers. Several human studies were also conducted.
   212	Vaithilingam _et al._ [ 71 ] compared the user experience of GitHub
   213	Copilot to traditional autocomplete in a user study and found that
   214	participants more frequently failed to complete tasks with Copilot,
   215	although there was no significant effect on task completion time.
   216	Barke [ 2 ] investicated further with a grounded theory analysis
   217	to understand _how_ programmers interact with code-generating
   218	models, using Github Copilot as an example. They identified two
   219	primary modes of interaction, acceleration or exploration, where
   220	Copilot is used to speed up code authoring in small logical units or
   221	as a planning assistant to suggest structure or API calls.
   222	Although these studies have increased our understanding of the
   223	usefulness and usability of AI programming assistants in general,
   224	and some of the insights apply to information support, they do
   225	not show the opportunities and challenges of LLM-based tools as
   226	information support tools, with a few following exceptions [ 45, 63 ].
   227	MacNeil et al. [ 45 ] examined the advantages of integrating code
   228	explanations generated by LLMs into an interactive e-book focused
   229	on web software development, with a user study with sophomores.
   230	They found students tend to find LLM-generated explanations to
   231	be useful, which is promising, but the study was focused on providing one-directional support in an introductory e-book which is
