   255	
   256	
   257	There are two ways to interact with the plugin. First, users can
   258	select parts of their code (Figure 1- 2 ) and trigger the tool by clicking on “AI Explanation” on the bottom bar (Figure 1- 1 ), or using
   259	
   260	
   261	Using an LLM to Help With Code Understanding ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal
   262	
   263	
   264	**Figure 1: Overview of our prototype. (1) A trigger button; (2) code used as context when prompting LLM; (3) code summary**
   265	**(no-prompt trigger); (4) buttons for further details; (5) an input box for user prompts; (6) options to embed information to code**
   266	**(Embed) and a hide/view button; (7) options to clear the panel (Clear all) and an abort LLM button; (8) a refresh button.**
   267	
   268	
   269	
   270	“alt/option + a” as a shortcut, to receive a summary description of
   271	the highlighted code ( Overview ). They can then explore further by
   272	clicking on buttons (Figure 1- 4 ) for API ( API ), domain-specific concepts ( Concept ), and usage examples ( Usage ), which provide more
   273	detailed explanations with preset prompts. The API button offers detailed explanations about the API calls used in the code, the Concept
   274	button provides domain-specific concepts that might be needed to
   275	understand the highlighted code fully, and the Usage button offers
   276	a code example involving API calls used in the highlighted code.
   277	Users can also ask a specific question directly to the LLM via the
   278	input box (Figure 1- 5 ). If no code is selected, the entire source code
   279	is used as context ( Prompt ); alternatively, the relevant code highlighted by the user is used ( Prompt-context ). The model will then
   280	answer the question with that code as context. GILT also allows
   281	users to probe the LLM by supporting conversational interaction
   282	( Prompt-followup ). When previous LLM-generated responses exist,
   283	if a user does not highlight any lines from the code, the LLM generates a response with the previous conversion as context. Users can
   284	also reset the context by triggering the tool with code highlighted,
   285	or with the Clear all button.
   286	
   287	
   288	**3.2** **Our Design Process and Decisions**
   289	
   290	
   291	**Focus on understanding.** We intentionally did not integrate a
   292	code generation feature in the prototype as we wanted to focus on
   293	how developers _understand_ code.
   294	
   295	
   296	
   297	**In-IDE extension.** Besides anticipating a better user experience,
   298	we designed the prototype as an in-IDE extension to more easily
   299	provide the code context to the LLM – participants could select
   300	code to use as part of the context for a query.
   301	
   302	
   303	**Pre-generated prompts.** We designed buttons that query the LLM
   304	with pre-generated prompts ( _prompt-less_ interaction) to ask about
   305	an API, conceptual explanations, or usage examples, as shown in
   306	Figure 1- 4 . We chose these based on API learning theory [ 14, 33,
   307	46, 68 ], expecting this may particularly assist novice programmers
   308	or those unfamiliar with the APIs/domains or the LLM, as writing
   309	efficient search queries or prompts can be difficult for novices [ 13,
   310	15, 32 ]. At the same time, we also expected that this could reduce
   311	the cognitive burden of users in general in formulating prompts.
   312	For Overview and the buttons API, Concept, Usage, we came up
   313	with prompt templates after a few iterations. To more efficiently
   314	provide the context to LLM, we used the library names and the
   315	list of API methods included in the selected code, such as “Please
   316	provide a [library name] code example, mainly showing the usage
   317	of the following API calls: [list of API methods]” for Usage .
   318	
   319	
   320	**Unrestricted textual queries.** Users can also directly prompt the
   321	LLM (Figure 1- 5 ), in which case GILT will automatically add any
   322	selected code as context for the query. Internally, the tool adds the
